bohmian-velocity-26a97dcb.interpretation:
The formula 
$$\mathbf{v} =  rac{\hbar}{m} 	ext{Im}  rac{
abla \psi}{\psi}
$$ defines the velocity vector 
$$\mathbf{v}
$$ of a quantum particle. Here, 
$$\hbar
$$ is the reduced Planck constant, a fundamental constant in quantum mechanics representing the quantum of angular momentum. 
$$\psi
$$ is the complex-valued wave function of the particle, which contains all the information about its quantum state. The term 
$$
abla \psi
$$ represents the gradient of the wave function, indicating the direction and rate of change of 
$$\psi
$$ in space. The ratio 
$$ rac{
abla \psi}{\psi}
$$ is a complex quantity. Taking the imaginary part, denoted by 
$$	ext{Im}
$$, extracts the component relevant to the flow of probability. This imaginary part is then scaled by 
$$ rac{\hbar}{m}
$, where 
$$\m
$ is the mass of the particle, to yield the instantaneous velocity 
$$\mathbf{v}
$$. This implies that the particle's motion is directly influenced by the spatial variations and phase of its wave function.

grid-density-36cb87aa.limits_and_boundary:
This relation is fundamental and does not have simple asymptotic limits in the same way classical physics equations do. It is a defining characteristic of quantum systems. However, in the classical limit (

    \(\hbar \to 0\)

    ), the commutator approaches zero (

    \([
    \hat{x}, \hat{p}
    \] \to 0\)

    ), which signifies that position and momentum can be simultaneously known with arbitrary precision, consistent with classical mechanics.
